+      2. SOLVE-RATE-PRESERVING LEVER CONTRACT (outer-loop 2026-06-20, MANDATORY -- this OVERRIDES any "commit the single candidate / filter / budget 1" phrasing): the adaptive budget MUST be a DEFER, not a DELETE. On a low-ambiguity ("commit") frame, move the top candidate to the FRONT of node['untested'] and KEEP THE TAIL in the list -- never remove a candidate from node['untested'] nor a node from the graph. `apply_adaptive_budget` returning [rows[0]] is a destructive filter that reproduces the A1/A2 knife-edge failure (a 5%-recall prune still dropped m0r0); return the FULL REORDERED list. Commit ONLY on re-VISITED frames (frame_is_novel=False), never novel frames. Solve-rate is then preserved BY CONSTRUCTION; the only question the experiment answers is whether front-loading cuts actions.
+      3. Measure median actions-to-first-levelup WITH vs WITHOUT the adaptive gate on the 8-game gate; report per-game + median + solve-rate. GATE = CORE set-containment over {lp85,m0r0,sp80,vc33} (NOT raw solved-count): report median_actions_on_core (median over the 4 CORE games, +inf for any CORE game not solved) and core_solves_preserved:bool. New solves outside CORE are a reported BONUS, never netted against a CORE loss. (The gate `_verdict` already implements this -- scripts/kaggle/arc_local_submission_gate.py.)
+      4. POSITIVE CONTROL + FALSE_NEGATIVE_RISK guard: if the gate does not save actions, report the HONEST NULL. Add committed_frame_fraction_on_winning_path -- if ~0 the lever is architecturally inert (it acts on per-frame fan-out width, the axis A1 proved moves actions <=0.3%); report the honest null, do NOT chase a 4<->3 flip.
       5. Solves stay offline-reproduced via arc_solver_kit.reproduce.
 
